                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font
                                    face="仿宋">shuminfeng@163.com</font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
                <td width=182 valign=top style="width:109.6500pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font
                                    face="仿宋">Dr. Shu Min-Feng, is an Associate Professor of Pharmacology and Virology in the Department of Pharmacology at Fudan University Shanghai Medical College, China. He earned his M.D. in 2006 and his Ph.D. in 2011. From 2011 to 2017, Dr. Shu was a postdoctoral scholar at the University of Chicago before joining the faculty at Fudan University in 2017. At Fudan, he has made significant contributions to the teaching of pharmacology, particularly in English, within the MBBS program. Dr. Shu's research focuses primarily on epigenetic mechanisms and intervention strategies for immune ignorance in the glioma microenvironment, with a particular emphasis on RNA modification, metabolism, and the use of herpes simplex virus (HSV) to reshape tumor-immune interactions. Using genetic manipulation models, molecular and biochemical techniques, and animal models, Dr. Shu has made groundbreaking discoveries regarding the regulation of oHSV oncolytic activity. Notably, his work has elucidated the underlying mechanisms by which RNA modification mediates therapeutic resistance to oHSV. Dr. Shu has been awarded numerous prestigious research grants, including the National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC) Special Key Project and the Program for Professor of Special Appointment (Eastern Scholar) at Shanghai Institutions of Higher Learning. With over 30 peer-reviewed publications, his work has appeared in leading journals such as Nature Communications, PNAS, Cell Reports, and others. Dr. Shu has received recognition from Faculty Opinions (F1000) and holds 12 international PCT and national invention patents.</font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
